Genealogy of Jesus Christ
A record containing the genealogy of Jesus Christ, the son of David, the son of Abraham.
Abraham was the father of Isaac. And Isaac was the father of Jacob. Jacob was the father of Judah and his brothers.
And Judas the father of Perez and Zerah. And their mother was Tamar. And Perez begat Hezron. And Hezron begat Ram.
Ram the father of Amminadab, and Amminadab the father of Nahshon, and Nahshon the father of Salmon.
Salmon was the father of Boaz. Rahab was the mother of Boaz. Boaz was the father of Obed. Ruth was the mother of Obed. Obed was the father of Jesse.
And Jesse was the father of King David. And David was the father of Solomon. And the mother of Solomon was the wife of Uriah.
Solomon the father of Rehoboam, Rehoboam the father of Abijah, Abijah the father of Asa.
Asa was the father of Jehoshaphat. And Jehoshaphat was the father of Joram. And Joram was the father of Uzziah.
Uzziah, the father of Joatham; and Joatham, the father of Ahaz; and Ahaz, the father of Hezekiah.
Hezekiah was the father of Manasseh. Manasseh was the father of Amon. Amon was the father of Josiah.
And Yehoiyakim was the father of Yehonatan and his brothers. At the time of their exile to Babylon.
And afterward the genealogy of the family continued. Now Hezera was the father of Shealtiel, and Shealtiel the father of Zerubbabel.
Zerubbabel was the father of Oded. Oded was the father of Eliakim. Eliakim was the father of Azra.
Azor is the father of Sadaq, and Sadaq is the father of Aqim, and Aqim is the father of Eilod.
Eliud was the father of Eliezer. Eliezer was the father of Mathai. Mathai was the father of Jacob.
Jacob is the father of Joseph. And Joseph was the husband of Mary. And Mary gave birth to Jesus who is called the Christ.
Fourteen generations were from Abraham to David; fourteen generations were from David until the Babylonian exile; fourteen generations were from there until the Christ. Jesus Christ was born.
This is how the birth of Jesus Christ happened. His mother Mary was engaged to Joseph. But before they started living together, it became clear that she would give birth to a child. Mary became pregnant through the power of the Holy Spirit.
Her husband, Yusuf, was a pious man. He did not want to expose her to shame before the people. Therefore, he planned to secretly divorce her.
But while he was thinking about this, an angel of the Lord appeared to him in a dream, saying, "Joseph, son of David, do not be afraid to take Mary your wife into your home. For the child conceived in her is from the Holy Spirit."
She will conceive a son. And you shall call his name Jesus, because he will save his people from their sins.
All this was done so that what the Lord had said would come to pass. For He spoke it through the mouth of the prophet.
A wife will bear a son and will call him by the name Immanuel, which means: God is with us.
Joseph woke up and did what the angel of the Lord had commanded him. He took Mary into his house as his wife.
But he did not know her until she gave birth to a son. And he called his name Jesus.
The coming of the wise men to visit Jesus.
Jesus was born in Bethlehem in the territory of Judea during the time of King Herod. And behold, an angel of the Lord appeared to him in a dream, saying, "Behold, I bring you good news of great joy that will be for all the people. For today in the city of David a Savior has been born to you; he is the Messiah, the Lord. This will be a sign for you: you will find a baby wrapped in cloths and lying in a manger." And at once there appeared with the angel a multitude of the heavenly army, praising God and saying, "Glory to God in the highest, and on earth peace among those whom he favors." And when the angels had left them and gone into heaven, the shepherds began saying to one another, "Let us go straight to Bethlehem and see this thing that has come to pass." And they went with haste and found Mary and Joseph, and the baby lying in the manger. And when they saw it, they made known the saying which was spoken to them concerning this child. And all who heard it marveled at the things which were spoken to them by the shepherds. But Mary treasured up all these things, pondering them in her heart. And the shepherds returned, glorifying and praising God for all the things they had heard and seen, which were just as they had been told. And all who heard of it marveled at the things which were told them by the shepherds.
They said, "Where is the king who has been born among the Jews? For we have seen his star in the east and have come to worship him."
When Herod the king heard the news, he was deeply disturbed; and all Jerusalem was disturbed with him.
Herod the king gathered the chief priests and scribes of the people in the same place, and asked them, "Where is the Messiah to be born?"
In Bethlehem, in Judea, they answered him. This is what the prophet wrote. He said
And as for you, Bethlehem Ephrathah, though you are small among the clans of Judah, yet out of you shall come one who will be a ruler of my people Israel,' says the Lord.
The king summoned the Magians secretly and questioned them about the time of the appearance of the star.
He sent them to Bethlehem and said, "Go and make a careful search for the child. And when you find him, report to me so that I may also go and worship him."
And after the wise men had listened to the king, they went on their way. And the brightness that they had seen in the East went before them, until finally it came to rest above the very place where the boy was.
When they saw the star, joy filled them.
The wise men went to the house. There they saw the child with his mother Mary. They prostrated to him and worshipped. Then they opened their treasures. They presented to him gold, frankincense, and myrrh.
And God warned them in a dream not to return to Herod, so they returned to their own country by another way. The family of Jesus fled to Egypt.
When the wise men had gone, an angel of the Lord appeared to Joseph in a dream, saying, "Get up, take the child and his mother, and flee to Egypt. Stay there until I tell you, for Herod is about to search for the child to kill him."
Youssef got up. And during the night, he went to Egypt with the child and his mother Mary.
They stayed there until King Herod died. Then what the Lord had said through the prophet came true: "Out of Egypt I have called my son." (Hosea 11:1)
When Herod realized that the Magi had deceived him, he became furiously angry and issued orders concerning Bethlehem and the entire surrounding region, to kill all the boy babies who were in the age bracket of two years or less. And this decree coincided with the time when the Magi had seen the star.
Thus were fulfilled the words which the prophet Jeremiah had spoken. For he had said,
A voice was heard in Ramah, lamenting and weeping, moaning and wailing. Rachel weeping for her children, refusing to be comforted because they were gone.
And after Herod's death, Joseph saw a dream in Egypt, and the angel of the Lord appeared to him in the dream.
The angel said, "Rise, take your child and his mother, and flee to the land of Israel. For those who sought to kill the child are dead."
Then Joseph took the child and his mother and returned with them to the land of Israel, where he was born.
But he heard that Herod the tetrarch had jurisdiction over Galilee, and that he had taken the place of his father Philip. Joseph was afraid to go there. In a dream he was instructed to go to the region of Galilee instead.
He lived there in a city called Nazareth. And thus, the prophecy of the prophets concerning Jesus was fulfilled. For they said, "He shall be called the Nazarene."
He makes the path right, John the Baptist.
In those days John the Baptist came, preaching in the wilderness of Judea.
He said, "Repent from your sins! For the Kingdom of Heaven is at hand."
Yahya is the one about whom Isaiah the prophet spoke, saying: "A voice is calling in the wilderness: 'Prepare the way for the Lord. Make his paths straight. Every valley shall be filled, and every mountain and hill shall be made low. The crooked roads shall become straight, and the rough paths smooth. Then all mankind shall see God's salvation.'" (Isaiah 40:3)
John's clothes were made of goat's hair. He wore a leather belt around his waist. His food was locusts and wild honey.
People came out to him from Jerusalem and all Judea, and from the region of the Jordan too.
When they confessed their sins, John baptized them in the River Jordan.
John saw many Pharisees and Sadducees coming to him for baptism, and he said to them: "O offspring of vipers, who warned you to flee from the wrath to come?"
You shall bear fruit that shows your repentance from your disobedience.
Do not suppose that you can say to yourselves, 'Abraham is our father.' I say to you, God is able from these very stones to raise up children to Abraham.
The axe now lies at the roots of the trees. All trees that do not bear good fruit will be cut down. And thrown into the fire.
I baptize you with water for repentance, but he who comes after me is mightier than I, whose sandals I am not worthy to carry. He will baptize you with the Holy Spirit and fire.
His paddle is in his hand to purify with it his oven from the straw. He gathers his wheat to the granary, and as for the straw, he burns it with an unquenchable fire." Jesus is baptized in the water.
Jesus came from Galilee to the Jordan. He wanted John to baptize him.
But John tried to prevent him. And he said to Jesus, "I need you to baptize me, so why are you coming to me?"
Jesus answered, "So let it be now, for it is expedient for us to do all that is right. To fulfill the will of the Holy God." John agreed to this.
And immediately when Jesus was baptized, he went up from the water. And at that time the heavens were opened, and Jesus saw the Spirit of God descending like a dove and alighting on him.
And a voice from heaven said: "This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased."
Jesus is tempted.
The Holy Spirit led Jesus into the wilderness. And there the devil tempted him.
And after forty days and forty nights of fasting, Jesus became hungry.
The tempter came and said to him, "If you are the Son of God, command these stones to become loaves of bread."
Jesus answered, "It is written: 'Man shall not live by bread alone, but by every word that proceeds from the mouth of God.'" (Deuteronomy 8:3)
Then the devil took Jesus to the holy city of Jerusalem, and he placed him on the pinnacle of the temple.
If you are the Son of God, throw yourself down, for it is written: 'He will command his angels concerning you to guard you closely; they will carry you in their hands, lest you strike your foot against a stone.' (Psalm 91:11, 12)
Jesus answered him, "It is written again, 'You shall not tempt the Lord your God.' " (Deuteronomy 6:16)
And finally, the devil took Jesus to a very high mountain. And he showed him all the kingdoms of the world and their glory.
He said, "If you prostrate to me and worship me, I will give you all of this."
Jesus said to him, "Get behind me, Satan! For it is written, 'You shall worship the Lord your God, and Him alone you shall serve.'" (Deuteronomy 6:13)
Then the devil left him, and behold, angels came and served him. When Jesus awoke from his solitary retreat, he began to preach, saying:
John was imprisoned. When Jesus heard about it, he returned to Galilee.
Jesus left Nazareth and resided in the city of Capernaum, which is located by the sea in the region of Zebulun and Naphtali.
Thus, what Isaiah the prophet spoke has come about.
The land of Zebulun and the land of Naphtali, the Galilee of the nations, a land at the Mediterranean Sea, across the Jordan.
Those who live in the land of deep darkness will see a great light. They live in a land of deep darkness, a land of shadows and darkness. A great light will shine upon you. (Isaiah 9:1,2)
From that time Jesus began to preach and say, "Repent, for the Kingdom of Heaven is at hand." And Jesus, turning around, saw them following Him, and He said to them, "Follow Me." And James and John, sons of Zebedee, on a quick glance, left their boat, their father, and followed Him.
One day Jesus walked along the shore of the Sea of Galilee. There he saw two brothers, Simon, who is called Peter, and his brother Andrew. They were casting a net into the sea, for they were fishermen.
Come and follow me, and I will make you fishers of people.
So they left the fish and followed him for a time.
He went from there and saw two others, James the son of Zebedee, and his brother John. They were in the boat with their father Zebedee, mending their nets. And Jesus called them.
A conquest, they hasten to the boat and left their father and followed Jesus. Healing the sick by the hand of Jesus.
Jesus went throughout Galilee. He was teaching in their synagogues, preaching the good news of the kingdom of God, and healing every disease and every affliction among the people.
News about him spread throughout Syria. And people were bringing to him all the sick people afflicted with various diseases. And some of them were suffering from excruciating pains, and some were possessed by demons, and some were shaking violently, and others were completely unable to move. And Jesus healed them all.
Great multitudes followed him. People came from Galilee, from the region of the Decapolis, and from Jerusalem and Judea. Others came from the region beyond the Jordan.
Jesus blesses.
Jesus saw the crowds. He went up on the mountain and sat down. His disciples came to him.
Then he began to teach them, saying:
Blessed are the pure in heart, for they shall see God.
Blessed are those who mourn, for they will be comforted.
Blessed are the meek, for they shall inherit the earth.
Blessed are the meek and those who hunger and thirst for justice, for they shall be satisfied.
Blessed are the merciful, for they shall obtain mercy.
Blessed are the pure in heart, for they shall see God.
Blessed are the peacemakers, for they shall be called the children of God.
Blessed are those who are persecuted for the sake of righteousness, for theirs is the kingdom of heaven.
Blessed are you when men revile you, and persecute you, and say all manner of evil against you falsely. Rejoice, and be exceeding glad for great is your reward in heaven. For so persecuted they the prophets which were before you.
Rejoice and be exceedingly glad, for great is your reward in heaven. Thus they persecuted the prophets who were before you. You are the salt of the earth and the light of the world.
You are the salt of the earth. But if the salt loses its taste, how can it be made salty again? It is no longer good for anything, but is thrown out and trampled under people's feet.
You are the light of the world. A city on a hill cannot be hidden.
Similarly, people do not light a lamp and put it under a bowl, but on a stand so that it may give light to everyone in the house.
In the same way, let your light shine before others, so that they may see your good works and give glory to your Father in heaven. Jesus fulfills the law by perfecting it.
Do not think that I have come to abolish the Law or the Prophets. I have not come to abolish but to fulfill.
What I am about to relate to you is a reality. Heaven and earth will not disappear before the smallest point of the Law disappears. Even the stroke of a pen will not disappear from the Law before the fulfillment of everything.
Do not nullify even one of the least commandments. And do not teach others to do so. For whoever does and teaches them will be called the least in the kingdom of heaven. But whoever does them and teaches them will be called great in the kingdom of heaven. For I tell you that unless your righteousness surpasses that of the scribes and Pharisees, you will never enter the kingdom of heaven. You have heard that it was said to those of old, 'You shall not murder. And anyone who commits murder must be put to death.' But I tell you that anyone who is angry with a brother or sister will be subject to judgment. And anyone who says to a brother or sister, 'Raca,' is answerable to the Sanhedrin. And anyone who says, 'You fool,' will be in danger of the fire of hell. Therefore, if you are offering your gift at the altar and there remember that your brother or sister has something against you, leave your gift there at the altar. First, go and be reconciled to your brother or sister. Then come and offer your gift. Settle matters quickly with your adversary who is taking you to court. Otherwise, your adversary may hand you over to the judge, and the judge may hand you over to the officer, and you may be thrown into prison. I tell you, you will not get out until you have paid the last penny.
This is what I tell you. You must be more righteous than the Pharisees and teachers of the law. For if you are not, you will not enter the kingdom of heaven. Murder
You have heard that it was said to the people long ago, 'You shall not murder.' (Exodus 20:13) 'Whoever murders will be subject to judgment.'
But this is what I'm telling you. Don't be angry with your brother. For whoever is angry with his brother will be subject to judgment. And whoever says to his brother 'Raca' will be subject to the greater judgment. And whoever says 'You fool' will be in danger of the fire of hell.
If then you are offering your gift at the altar, and there you remember that your brother has something against you,
Leave your gift before the altar. First, go and be reconciled with your brother, and then come and offer your gift.
Let there be for you against your hater the same as for you against him, until you become dust. And if you were on an even path, they would deny the Day of Judgment.
What I am going to tell you now is the truth. You will not leave here before you have paid the last penny! Adultery.
You have heard that it was said, "You shall not commit adultery" (Exodus 20:14).
But I say to you: whoever looks at a woman to desire her has already committed adultery with her in his heart.
If your right eye causes you to stumble, tear it out and throw it away. It is better for you to lose one part of your body than for your whole body to be thrown into hell.
Indeed, if your right hand rises to commit sin, cut it off and cast it away from you. For the loss of a part of you is better than the loss of your entire body in the Fire. Divorce
It was said, "If a man divorces his wife, he shall give her a certificate of divorce." (Deuteronomy 24:1)
But this is what I tell you: whoever divorces his wife, except for sexual immorality, makes her commit adultery; and whoever marries a divorced woman commits adultery. But I say to you that whoever divorces his wife, except for sexual immorality, causes her to commit adultery; and if she marries another, she commits adultery. But I say to you that whoever divorces his wife, except for sexual immorality, causes her to commit adultery; and if she marries another, she commits adultery. Let what you say be simply 'Yes' or 'No'; anything more than this comes from evil.
And again, you have heard that the ancients were warned by the prophets who spoke to them; as it is written of them, "Do not forsake the covenant with the Lord your God, and do not regard vain oracles, for the Lord your God is the one who gives you true oracles."
But that's what I'm telling you. Never make promises like that. Don't take heaven as a witness to your promises. For that is God's throne.
Do not make it in the name of the Earth. That is the footstool of God. Do not make it in the name of Jerusalem. That is the city of the Great King.
And do not swear by your head that white hair will become black or become white.
Say the truth; I am not a polytheist; I am a worshiper of the Most Merciful; so worship whatever you want beside Him; say, "The truth is from your Lord, so whoever wills - let him believe; and whoever wills - let him disbelieve"; indeed, We have prepared for the disbelievers shackles and a blazing fire; indeed, to Allah belongs the dominion of the heavens and the earth; and on the Day when the Hour is established, the criminals will be in despair; and they will have no intercessor from among the messengers; and you will see the criminals that day bound together in chains; their garments of fire; over it are piles of pitch; whenever they try to escape from it, they will be returned to it, and they will be told, "Taste the punishment of the Fire, which you used to disbelieve in." Indeed, We will give the good things in this world to those who believe and do righteous deeds, and to those who love others as they love themselves; indeed, to Allah belongs the dominion of the heavens and the earth, and to Allah belongs all matters; and those who have believed and emigrated and fought for the cause of Allah and those who have given aid and shelter to them - these, too, are believers in truth; for them are forgiveness and noble provision; and those who believed after them and emigrated and fought for the cause of Allah, and for them who gave aid and shelter to them - these, too, are believers in truth; for them are forgiveness and noble provision; this is guidance from Allah; and for whom Allah guides - then he is the one led aright; and for those whom you ask for a fee - they are not listeners; and even if they were listeners, they would not be able to understand; and even if they were able to understand, they would not have accepted it; and even if they had accepted it, they would not have acted upon it; and even if they had acted upon it, they would not have persisted upon it; and even if they had persisted upon it, it would not have been accepted from them; and even if it had been accepted from them, it would not have been rewarded from them; and even if it had been rewarded from them, it would not have been increased for them; and even if it had been increased for them, it would not have been blessed for them; and even if it had been blessed for them, it would not have been for them; and even if it had been for them, it would not have been from them; and even if it had been from them, it would not have been for them; and even if it had been for them, it would not have been from them; and even if it had been from them, it would not have been for them; and even if it had been for them, it would not have been from them; and even if it had been from them, it would not have been for them; and even if it had been for them, it would not have been from them; and even if it had been from them, it would not have been for them; and even if it had been for them, it would not have been from them; and even if it had been from them, it would not have been for them; and even if it had been for them, it would not have been from them; and even if it had been from them, it would not have been for them; and even if it had been for them, it would not have been from them; and even if it had been from them, it would not have been for them; and even if it had been for them, it would not have been from them
You have heard that it was said, 'An eye for an eye and a tooth for a tooth.' (Exodus 21:24, Leviticus 24:20, Deuteronomy 19:21)
But that is what I am telling you. Do not resist the evil one. "If someone strikes you on the right cheek, turn to him the other cheek also.
If someone takes you to court to take your shirt, let them take your coat as well.
If anyone is compelled against you, they should go along with him for a stage. So go along with him for two stages.
Give to the one who asks from you, and do not deprive the one who wants to borrow from you. Love your enemies.
You have heard that it was said, "Love your neighbor" (Leviticus 19:18) and "hate your enemy."
But I tell you, Love your enemies, pray for those who mistreat you.
Then you will be sons of your Father who is in the heavens. For He causes His sun to rise on the evil and the good, and sends rain on the just and the unjust.
If you love those who love you, what reward will you get? Even the tax collectors do that.
If you are only greeting your family, what more are you doing than what others are doing? Even irreligious people do that.
Be you, then, perfect even as your heavenly Father is perfect.
Give to those in need.
Beware of doing 'good works' before people so that they may see you. Do not do them so that others may glorify you. For if you do that, your Father who is in heaven will not reward you.
When you give to the needy, do not sound a trumpet, as the hypocrites do in the synagogues and in the streets, to be seen by men. I tell you the truth: they have received their reward.
If you give to the poor, let not your left hand know what your right hand is spending.
Then pray a secret prayer. Your father, who sees in secret, he is the one who rewards you.
When you pray, do not be like the hypocrites, for they love to pray standing in the synagogues and at the street corners, so that they may be seen by others. I tell you the truth, they have received their reward in full.
When you pray, go into your room, close the door, and pray to your Father who is in secret; for your Father who sees in secret will reward you.
When you pray, do not babble on and on like the pagans do. For they imagine that they will be heard for their much speaking.
Do not be like them, for your Father knows what you need before you ask Him.
Thus they prayed: "Our Father who art in heaven, Hallowed be thy name"
Your kingdom come, your will be done, on earth as it is in heaven.
Give us this day our daily bread.
Forgive us our trespasses, as we forgive those who trespass against us.
Deliver us from evil when we are tempted. Lead us not into temptation. Save us from the evil one.
Forgive people and your father in heaven will forgive you.
And if you do not forgive people their slips, your Lord will not forgive your slips either. Fasting.
When you fast and do not eat, do not look with a frown like those who pretend to be pious. They make their faces into stages for sadness, wanting to show people that they are fasting. What I am about to tell you now is truly truth. They have received their full reward.
But if you fast, anoint your heads and wash your faces, that you may not be seen fasting by men, but by your Father who is in secret, and your reward will be great in heaven.
Furthermore, it is your duty to conceal your fasting, so that no one knows about it, except your heavenly Father, who is invisible. He alone knows what you do in secret, and He will reward you for it. Your heavenly Father, who sees in secret, is the One who rewards you. Pay no attention to those who claim to be delivering your money to the heavens.
Do not gather a treasure for yourselves in the earth, for the moth will corrupt it, and the rust will stain it, and the thieves will strip it.
Instead, lay up for yourselves treasures in heaven, where neither moth nor rust destroys, and where thieves do not break in or steal.
Your heart will be where your treasure is.
The eye is like a bright lamp in the body; and if your eyes are enlightened, your whole body will be bright.
But if your eye is bad, your whole body will be full of darkness. If the light within you is darkness, how great is that darkness!
No one can serve two masters at the same time. Either you will hate the one and love the other, or you will be devoted to the one and despise the other. You cannot serve both God and money. Therefore, I tell you not to worry about your life, what you will eat or drink, or about your body, what you will wear. Is not life more than food, and the body more than clothes? Look at the birds of the air; they do not sow or reap or gather into barns, yet your heavenly Father feeds them. Are you not much more valuable than they? Which of you by worrying can add a single hour to his life? Since you cannot do even a very little thing, why do you worry? The lamps of the body are the eyes. If your eyes are good, your whole body will be full of light. But if your eyes are bad, your whole body will be full of darkness. Therefore, if the light that is in you is darkness, how great is that darkness! No one can serve two masters; for either he will hate the one and love the other, or he will be devoted to the one and despise the other. You cannot serve God and wealth. Therefore I tell you, do not be anxious about your life, what you will eat or drink; nor about your body, what you will put on. Is not life more than food, and the body more than clothing? Look at the birds of the air; they neither sow nor reap, nor gather into barns; yet your heavenly Father feeds them. Are you not much more valuable than they? Which of you by being anxious can add a single hour to his life? Since you cannot do even a very little thing, why do you have such great anxiety? The lamps of the body are the eyes. If your eyes are good, your whole body will be full of light. But if your eyes are bad, your whole body will be full of darkness. Therefore, if the light that is in you is darkness, how great is that darkness! A good man brings good things out of the store of his heart, and an evil man brings evil things out of the store of his heart. For the mouth speaks what the heart is full of. A good man out of the good treasure of his heart brings forth good; and an evil man out of the evil treasure of his heart brings forth evil. For where your treasure is, there your heart will be also. No one can serve two masters; for either he will hate the one and love the other, or he will be devoted to the one and despise the other. You cannot serve God and wealth. Therefore I tell you, do not be anxious about your life, what you will eat or drink; nor about your body, what you will put on. Is not life more than food, and the body more than clothing? Look at the birds of the air; they neither sow nor reap, nor gather into barns; yet your heavenly Father feeds them. Are you not much more valuable than they? Which of you by being anxious can add a single hour to his life? Since you cannot do even a very little thing, why do you have such great anxiety? The lamps of the body are the eyes. If your eyes are good, your whole body will be full of light. But if your eyes are bad, your whole body will be full of darkness. Therefore, if the light that is in you is darkness, how great is that darkness! No one can serve two masters; for either he will hate the one and love the other, or he will be devoted to the one and despise
I tell you: Do not worry about your life, what you will eat or drink, and do not worry about your body, what you will wear. Is not life more than food, and the body more than clothing?
Look at the birds of the sky, they do not sow nor do they reap, nor do they gather into barns, and your heavenly Father feeds them. Are you not much better than they?
Can you add one hour to your life by worrying?
And why do you care about clothing? Look at the field's flower how it grows. It does not get tired and does not spin, 10 but I tell you that even Solomon himself in all his glory was not dressed like one of them.
But that is what I am telling you. Even Solomon in all his glory was not arrayed like one of those flowers.
If this is the manifestation of God to the grass that grows in the field, and tomorrow it will be thrown into the oven, does not God much improve your dress? O ye of little faith!
Therefore do not be anxious, saying, 'What shall we eat?' or 'What shall we drink?' or 'What shall we wear?'
The wicked pursue all these things. Your heavenly Father knows that you need them.
But first seek his kingdom and his righteousness, and all these things will be added to you.
Therefore, do not be anxious about tomorrow, for tomorrow will be anxious for itself. Sufficient for the day is its own distress.
Be just when you make the mind judge in the other.
Do not judge others, and you will not be judged.
You will be judged by the same standard that you judge others. You will be measured by the same measure that you measure others.
You look at the speck of sawdust in your friend's eye, but you don't notice the beam of wood in your own eye.
How can you say to your friend: "Let me take the piece of wood out of your eye"? How can you say this when you are holding a piece of wood in your own eye?
O accuser! First, take the beam out of your own eye. Then, you will see clearly and be able to remove the splinter from your friend's eye.
Do not give the Jerusalemites to the dogs, and do not print your pearls before the swine, lest they trample them under their feet and look back and tear you open. Ask and it will be given to you, seek and you will find, knock and it will be opened to you.
Ask and you shall receive. Seek and you shall find. Knock and the door shall be opened for you.
He who asks shall receive. He who seeks shall find. The door will be opened to those who knock.
Let's suppose that your son is asking for bread. Which one of you would give him a stone?
Or if he asked for a fish, would any of you give him a snake?
And if you yourself are evil, you know that you should give good gifts to your children, so how much more will your Father in heaven give good gifts to those who ask Him!
In everything do to others as you would have them do to you. This is written in the law and the prophets. In the great and small entrances
Enter God's kingdom through the narrow gate. For the gate is wide and the road is easy that leads to destruction and hell. And many are those who go in it.
But the entrance is narrow and the path is rough that leads to life. And few are those who find it. The tree and its fruit
Beware of false prophets, which come to you in sheep's clothing, but inwardly they are ravening wolves.
You can know who they are by what they do. Do people pick grapes from thorns? Or do they gather figs from thistles?
Likewise, every good tree bears good fruit. But a bad tree bears bad fruit.
A good tree does not produce bad fruit, and a bad tree does not produce good fruit.
Every tree that does not bear good fruit is cut down and thrown into the fire.
Every tree is known by its fruit.
Not everyone who says to me, 'Lord, Lord,' will enter the kingdom of heaven, but the one who does the will of my Father who is in heaven.
Many people say to me on that day: Lord, Lord! Is it not in your name that we prophesied, and in your name that we cast out demons, and in your name that we performed miracles?
Then I will tell them plainly: "I never knew you! Away from me, you evildoers!" The wise and the ignorant are in buildings.
Therefore everyone who hears these words of mine and does them will be like a wise man who built his house on the rock.
The rain falls. The water rises. The wind blows and strikes that house. But it does not fall. For it was built on the rock.
But whoever hears my words and does not act on them is like the foolish man who built his house on the sand.
The rain falls. The water rises. The wind blows and slaps that house. And it collapses with a loud, thunderous sound.
Jesus finished all this talk of His. And the crowds were amazed at His teaching.
He spoke with authority, not like our rabbis.
Jesus heals a man with a skin disease.
Jesus came down from the mountain, and a great crowd followed him.
A man with a loathsome disease came and fell on his knees before Jesus, saying, "Lord, if you are willing, you can make me clean."
Jesus stretched out his hand and touched the man, saying, "I want to do this. Save yourself, you unclean one!" And immediately the leprosy was cleansed from him.
Then Jesus said to him, "Do not say this to anyone, but go and show yourself to the priest and offer the sacrifice for your cleansing that Moses commanded as a witness to them." The Faith of a Roman Centurion.
When Jesus entered Capernaum, a Roman centurion came forward and asked him for help.
He said: "O my lord, my slave sits confined to the house, suffering extreme hardship."
Jesus said, "I will go and heal him."
The captain replied, "Sir, I am not worthy for you to enter my house, but with your word, my servant will be healed."
I myself am a man under authority and have soldiers who obey my orders. I tell this one, "Go," and he goes. And to that one I say, "Come," and he comes. And I say to my servant, "Do this," and he does it.
Jesus was astonished at his words and said to those who were following him: "Truly I tell you, I have not found anyone in all of Israel who has such great faith!
I tell you, many will come from the east and the west, and will take their place at the feast with Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob in the Kingdom of Heaven.
But the ones who think that they are from the kingdom of God will be cast out into the outer darkness. There will be weeping and gnashing of teeth.
Then Jesus said to the Roman centurion: "Go, and as you believed, so let it be done for you." And immediately the boy was healed.
When Jesus entered Peter's house, he saw Peter's mother-in-law lying in bed, suffering from a fever.
Jesus touched her hand, and the fever left her immediately. She rose and served him.
When evening came, many who had demons inside them were brought to Jesus. All the possessed people were brought to him, and he healed them all.
He did it to fulfill what the prophet Isaiah said: "He bore our sins and our sorrows; He bore them all, yet we thought He was sick, struck down by God, and utterly humiliated." (Isaiah 53:4) The price of following Jesus is exorbitant.
Jesus saw the crowd around him, so he instructed his disciples to sail to the other side, to the region of the Ten Cities.
Then a scribe from the scribes came to him. And he said to him, "I will go where you go also, I, O teacher."
Jesus answered, "Foxes have dens and birds of the air have nests, but the Son of Man has no place to lay his head."
Another follower said to him, "Sir, first let me go and bury my father."
But Jesus said to him, "Follow Me, and let the dead bury their dead." Jesus calms the storm.
Jesus entered the boat. The disciples followed him.
Suddenly a terrible storm blew over the lake, and the waves rose high to break against the boat. But Jesus was sleeping.
His disciples came to Him and woke Him up, saying, "Teacher, save us! We are perishing!"
He said, "You of little faith, why were you so afraid?" And He got up and rebuked the winds and the sea, and it became perfectly calm. The men were amazed, saying, "What kind of man is this, that even the winds and the sea obey him?"
The disciples were astonished and said, "What kind of man is this? Even the winds and the waves obey him!" Jesus healing two demon-possessed men.
Jesus crossed over the sea to the district of the Gerasenes. When he stepped ashore, a man from the tombs, possessed by demons, met Jesus. The man was cutting himself with stones. Jesus commanded the unclean spirit to leave the man. The man fell down before Jesus and confessed, "I am not worthy to remain under your roof. Make me like one of your hired men." Jesus said to him, "Go home to your family and tell them how much the Lord has done for you, and has had mercy on you." The man left to tell his family about the great things Jesus had done for him.
O son of God, what do you want from us? Have you come to torment us before the time of our judgment?
It was not very far, a large group of pigs was grazing.
The demons pleaded with Jesus, saying, "If you want to drive us out, send us into the herd of pigs."
Jesus said to them, "Go." So the demons came out of the two men and entered the pigs, and the herd of pigs rushed down the steep bank into the lake and were drowned.
The pig keepers hurried to the village and told the people everything that had happened, and how the men who had been possessed by demons had been freed.
Then the whole city went out to meet Jesus. When they saw him, they pleaded with him to leave their region.
Jesus Heals a Paralyzed Man
Jesus got into a boat and crossed over to the other side of the lake, and he came to his own town.
He said to him, "Do not despair, my son, your sins are forgiven you."
Some of the clerks and Pharisees said to themselves, "This man is blaspheming!"
Jesus knew what was in their hearts, so he said to them, "Why are you thinking evil in your hearts?"
Is it easier to say, "Your sins have been forgiven," or to say, "Rise up and walk"?
I want you to know that the human being's son has authority over the earth to forgive sins." Then He said to the sick man: "Get up, take up your bed and walk." Immediately the man was healed, took up his bed, and walked out.
The man got up and went to his house.
When the crowd saw this, they were amazed and glorified God, who had given such authority to people. They called for Jesus and said, "We praise the God of heaven who has given you the authority to perform miraculous signs and wonders."
Jesus went on from there and saw a man called Matthew sitting at the tax office. And he said to him, "Follow me." And he rose and followed him.
Jesus was later reclining in the house of Matthew. And many tax collectors and sinners came and were reclining with him and his disciples.
The Pharisees saw this and said to the disciples, "Why does your teacher eat with tax collectors and sinners?"
Jesus heard this. And he said, "It is not those who are well who need a physician, but those who are ill."
Go and learn what the meaning of the words are: "I desire mercy and not sacrifice." (Hosea 6:6) For I did not come to call the righteous, but sinners, to repentance."
One day, the disciples of John came to him and said, "Rabbi, we and the Pharisees fast often, yet your disciples do not fast."
Jesus answered, "How can the groomsmen mourn as long as the bridegroom is with them? As long as the bridegroom is still with them, they cannot mourn. But the time is coming when I will be taken from them, and at that time they will fast."
People do not sew a new piece of cloth onto old clothes. The new piece would pull and stretch the old. That would further ruin the hole.
People do not pour new wine into old wine skins. Otherwise, the skins will burst, the wine will spill out, and the skins will be ruined. Instead, they pour new wine into new skins. Then, the skins do not burst, the wine does not spill, and the skins remain intact.
While he was still speaking, a ruler came and knelt before him, saying, "My daughter has just now died, but come and lay Your hand on her, and she will live."
Jesus rose up, and Mary followed him, and the disciples also followed him.
And immediately a woman who had been suffering from a hemorrhage for twelve years came up behind him and touched the hem of his garment.
She thought, "It is enough for me to touch his clothes and be healed."
And Jesus turned and saw her, and said to her, "Woman, your faith has healed you." And the woman was healed in that moment.
When Jesus entered the chief priest's house, he looked at the comforters, the singers, and the confused crowd.
He told them, "Go away, the girl is not dead, she is sleeping." But they laughed at him.
And after he had put the crowd outside, he took the hand of the young girl, and she immediately arose.
News of what Jesus did spread throughout the region. Jesus healing two blind men.
And as the Messiah walked afterward, two blind men followed him, crying out and saying, "Have mercy on us, O son of David!"
When Jesus entered the house, the blind approached him. He said to them, "Do you believe that I am able to do this?" They said to him, "Yes, Lord!"
Then he touched their eyes and said, "According to your faith, be it done to you."
Their eyes are now opened, and they see, and Jesus warned them severely, saying, "Behold, tell no one about this."
But they went out and spread the news. They talked about it everywhere in that region.
And while they are going out, they present to him another man, demon-possessed, and he is screaming and cannot speak.
And when the devil was cast out, the man spoke. The multitude were astonished and said, "We never saw anything like this in Israel."
But the Freemasons said: "With the power of the chief of the devils, the devils are cast out." Few are the doers.
Jesus went throughout all the cities and villages, teaching in their synagogues, and preaching the gospel of the kingdom, and healing all who were sick and diseased.
When he saw the crowds, he had compassion for them, because they were like sheep without a shepherd.
Then Jesus said to his disciples: "The harvest is plentiful, but the laborers are few."
So, seek from the Lord of the harvest to send out laborers into his harvest.
Jesus sent the twelve disciples, commanding them.
Jesus called his twelve disciples to him. He gave them authority to drive out all demons and to heal every disease and illness.
These are the names of the twelve apostles. First, Simon Peter and his brother Andrew. Then James the son of Zebedee and his brother John.
The following are Philip and Bartholomew, and also Thomas and Matthew the tax collector. And two others are James the son of Alphaeus and Judas the Zealot.
The last of them were Simon the Zealot and Judas Iscariot. Judas is the one who will later betray Jesus to his enemies.
Jesus sent the twelve out with clear instructions: "Do not go to the Gentiles, and do not enter any Samaritan town.
Go instead to the house of Israel. For they are like lost sheep.
And they went forth, saying, "The kingdom of the heavens has drawn near."
Heal the sick. Raise the dead to life. Cleanse the lepers. Drive out the demons. You received freely, so give freely.
Do not carry gold or silver or bronze in your belts.
Do not take a suitcase for travel. Do not carry extra clothes, sandals, or walking sticks. The worker should be provided with what they need.
When you enter a city or a village, look for someone who will receive you as guests. Stay in his house until you leave.
In the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful. When you enter the house, greet its people.
If they extend to you friendship, then enter into it with them and turn to your Lord, and for those who transgress limits against you, only speak to them a kind word and say to them, "Peace."
Not everyone will receive you or listen to your words. And if they do not, shake off the dust of your feet and leave that house or city.
As soon as I tell it to you, you have the right. On the Day of Reckoning, the people of Sodom and Gomorrah will be more merciful than the people of that city.
I send you out as sheep among wolves. Therefore be wise as serpents and harmless as doves.
Beware! The men will deliver you to the local courts. They will scourge you in their assemblies.
You will be brought before governors and kings because of me. You will be witnesses for me in Jerusalem, and in all Judea and Samaria, and to the end of the earth.
But when they deliver you up to the courts, do not be anxious beforehand about what you are to say, for it will be given you in that hour what to speak.
It will not be you who is speaking, but the Spirit of your Father speaking through you.
Brothers are handing over their brothers to be killed, and fathers their children. And sons rise up against their parents and kill them.
Everyone will hate him because of me. But the one who endures to the end will be saved.
When people attack you somewhere, run away to another place. What I am telling you now is true: you will not finish surrounding all the cities of Israel until the Son of Man comes.
The student is not better than his teacher. And the servant is not better than his master.
It is enough for the disciple to be like his master, and the servant like his lord. If the head of this house has been called Beelzebul, what else will they call the rest of those who live in this house?
Do not fear your enemy. For everything that is secret will be revealed, and everything that is hidden will be known.
What I am telling you in the darkness, speak of it in the light of day. And what is whispered in your ear, proclaim it from the rooftops.
Do not be afraid of those who kill the body and after that have nothing else they can do. But I will show you whom you should fear: fear the one who, after the body has been destroyed, has the power to throw both body and soul into the fires of hell.
Isn't it two sparrows sold for a farthing? And it is not that a sparrow falls to the ground without your Father.
He even counts each single hair on your head!
Fear not, for you are more honorable than a great multitude of sparrows.
What about someone who says in front of others that they know me? I will also say in front of my Father who is in heaven that I know him.
But what about the one who says before people, 'I do not know you'? I will say before my Father who is in heaven, 'I do not know you.'
Do not suppose that I have come to bring peace to the earth. I did not come to bring peace, but a sword.
I have come to incite the son against his father, and the daughter against her mother, and the daughter-in-law of a woman against her mother-in-law.
Enemies of man are those of his household. (Micah 7:6)
Whoever loves his father or his mother more than me does not deserve me; and whoever loves his son or his daughter more than me does not deserve me.
And whoever does not take up his cross and follow me cannot be my disciple.
Whoever finds his life will lose it, and whoever loses it for my sake will find it.
He who accepts you accepts me, and he who accepts me accepts the one who sent me.
Indeed, whoever welcomes a prophet like the prophets will receive the reward of a prophet. And whoever welcomes the pious like the pious, then he will receive the reward of the pious.
Suppose someone offers, even if only one cup of cold water, to a little one who follows me, I tell you truly: he will be rewarded.
Jesus and John the Baptist.
Jesus finished teaching his twelve disciples, and then he moved on to teachings and preaching in the cities of Galilee.
John was in prison. When he heard about the works of Christ, he sent his disciples to him.
They asked Jesus, "Are you the one who is to come, or shall we look for another?"
Jesus answered and said to them, "Go and tell John the things you hear and see.
The blind see, the lame walk, the lepers are cleansed, the deaf hear, the dead rise, and the poor have good news preached to them.
Blessed are those who do not walk in darkness because of me.
"What did you go out into the wilderness to see? A reed shaken by the wind?"
So what did you go out to see? A man dressed in fine clothes? No. For those who wear fine clothes are in palaces of the ancestors.
What then have you come out to see? A prophet? Yes, more than a prophet.
He is the one written about in the books: "I will send my angel, who will prepare the way before you" (Malachi 3:1)
What I am about to tell you is the truth. No one who has ever lived is greater than John the Baptist. But the least important person in the Kingdom of Heaven is greater than he was.
For days, the Queen of Heaven has been moving forward with might, and the powerful snatch her.
He spoke through the prophets and the Law, all of them until John.
If you are willing to accept that, John is the Yahya who was designated to come.
Let those who hear, hear.
What is most similar to people today is children who sit in the market calling out to each other.
We left you to burst into songs. But you did not dance. We sang you a lament. But you did not weep.
When John came, he did not eat or drink, as you do. And people used to say, "He has a demon in him."
But when the Son of Man came, he ate and drank, and they say, 'This man is a glutton and a drunkard, a friend of tax collectors and sinners.' And wisdom is vindicated by her deeds." Cities that do not repent.
Jesus began to speak out against the cities where He had done most of His miracles. For their people did not repent of their sins. And He said,
Woe to you, Chorazin! Woe to you, Bethsaida! For if the miracles had been done in Tyre and Sidon which were done in you, they would have repented long ago, sitting in sackcloth and ashes.
But I tell you: it will be more tolerable on the day of judgment for Tyre and Sidon than for you.
And you, Capernaum, will you be exalted to heaven? No, but you will be brought down to Hades! For if the miracles had been performed in Sodom which had been performed in you, it would have remained until this day.
But I tell you that the people of Sodom will be in a better situation on the day of judgment than you will be. Blessed rest for all who are weary.
At that time Jesus said, "You are my praise, O Father, Lord of heaven and earth, because you have hidden these things from the wise and learned and revealed them to the little children."
Yes, Dad. This is what you wanted.
My father gave me everything. And the Father alone knows the Son. And the Son reveals Him to whom He will reveal Him.
Come unto me, all ye that labor and are heavy laden, and I will give you rest.
Become my slaves and learn from me, for I am gentle and free from pride. You will find rest for yourselves.
My service is easy and my download is light.
Jesus is the Lord of the day, Saturday.
On Saturday, Jesus was walking in the field. And his disciples were also harvesting the wheat while grinding it with their hands.
Therefore the Pharisees saw it, and they said to Jesus: "Behold, your disciples do what is not lawful to do on the Sabbath."
Jesus answered, "Have you not even read what David did when he was hungry, he and his men?"
He entered the house of God, he and his men, and they ate the holy bread, which was only permitted to be eaten by the priests.
Have you not read the book? For it is written before you that the work shall be required on the sabbath of the priests in the temple. And they are guiltless.
I am telling you that there is someone who is greater than the temple here.
The text says: "I desire mercy, not sacrifice" (Hosea 6:6). You do not know what these two words mean. If you knew, you would not accuse the innocent.
And Christ will call out: "Allah is my Lord and your Lord."
Proceeding from there, Jesus entered their synagogue.
There was a man whose hand was withered and deformed. The Pharisees asked Jesus, saying, "Is it lawful to heal on the Sabbath?"
He said to them, "Suppose one of your sheep falls into a well on the Sabbath? Wouldn't you immediately pull it out and free it?"
A man is better than sheep! Therefore the law allows us to do good on the Sabbath day.
Then Jesus said to the man, "Stretch out your hand." He stretched it out, and it was restored, just as sound as the other.
But the Pharisees went out and conspired to kill Jesus. Abdullah the Chosen One
Jesus knew all things within the grasp of the Pharisees, so he departed from there. A great multitude followed him, and he healed all the sick people of the common people.
But he warned them not to reveal his identity.
So that what was spoken by the tongue of Isaiah the prophet might be fulfilled:
He is my servant whom I have chosen, my beloved in whom I delight. I will put my Spirit upon him, and he will proclaim the nations the truth.
He will not argue or shout. No one will hear his voice in the streets.
He will not break a bending branch. He will not extinguish a dim flame. He will fix everything.
The nations shall put their hope in him." (Isaiah 42:1-4) Jesus and Beelzebub
A man who had been seized by a demon was brought to Jesus. This man was blind and mute. Jesus healed him, and the man spoke and saw.
All the people were amazed and said, "Is this the Son of David?"
When the Pharisees heard this, they said, "He is casting out demons by the prince of demons, Beelzebul."
Jesus knew what was in their hearts, so he said to them, "Every kingdom that is divided against itself will be ruined, and every city or house that is divided against itself will not stand."
If Satan casts out Satan, he is at war with himself. So how can his kingdom stand?
You say that I cast out demons by the power of Belzebub. By what power then do your disciples cast them out? Let them rather judge you.
But if you cast out demons by the power of God's Spirit, then the kingdom of God has come upon you.
Or think of it like this: How could you break into a strong man's house and take what he owns? You would have to tie him up first. Then you could plunder his house.
Whoever is not with me is against me, and whoever does not gather with me scatters.
Therefore here is what I tell you. Every sin and every blasphemy against God can be forgiven. Blasphemy against the Holy Spirit will not be forgiven.
He who speaks a word against the Son of Man will be forgiven; but he who speaks against the Holy Spirit will not be forgiven, either in this age or in the age to come.
If you make the tree good, its fruit will be good. If you make it bad, its fruit will be bad. You can know the tree by its fruit.
Oh, nest of poisonous snakes! How can you say something good when you are evil?! Your mouths speak what is in your hearts.
The kind man says good things. They stem from the goodness hidden within him. The evil man says evil things. They stem from the evil hidden within him.
But behold, I tell you: On the day of judgment, people will be held accountable for every idle word they have spoken.
"In your words you will be condemned or acquitted." The sign of the miraculous Jonah
Some Pharisees and teachers of the law came to Jesus and said to him, "Teacher, we want to see a sign from you."
He answered them, saying: "He seeks a sign for the statement, the evil ones are not believers! But no sign is given to them except the sign of Jonah the prophet."
Jonah was in the belly of a large fish three days and three nights. So shall the Son of Man be in the heart of the earth three days and three nights.
The men of Nineveh in the district are doing this with this present generation, and they condemn them; for they have repented of their sins after Jonah preached to them. And here is someone greater than Jonah, right here!
The Queen of the South will rise on the day of judgment with this generation, present-day people, and will condemn them. She came from the ends of the earth to hear the wisdom of Solomon, and now he who is greater than Solomon has come.
What happens when an evil spirit comes out of a person? It wanders about in dry places, looking for a place to rest. But it finds none.
Then he says, "I will return to my house from which I went out." And when he returns, he finds it empty, cleaned, and tidy.
Then the evil spirit returns and takes with him seven other spirits more wicked than itself, and they enter and dwell there. And that man's condition in the end is worse than what it was in the beginning. And so it will be also for this evil generation." The mother of Jesus and his brothers.
And at the very time when Jesus was addressing the crowd, His mother and His brothers and sisters were standing outside, wishing to speak to Him.
Someone told him: "Your mother and your brothers are standing outside. They would like to talk to you."
Jesus answered him, "Who is my mother and who are my brothers?"
And Jesus pointed to his disciples, saying, "Behold my mother and my brethren!"
Whoever does the will of my Father who is in heaven, he is my brother and sister and mother.
The story of the farmer.
On the same day, Jesus left the house and sat on the shore of the Sea of Galilee.
Large crowds gathered around him, so he boarded a boat and sat in it. All the people stood on the shore.
Then he told them their stories, saying, "A farmer went out to sow his seed.
He scattered the seed on the ground. And some of it fell on the road, and the birds came and ate it.
Some of the seed fell on rocky places, where there was little soil. And the plants grew quickly because the surface soil was thin.
When the sun rose, it burned the plants. They dried up because they had no roots.
Other seed fell among the thorns; and the thorns grew up and choked the plants.
And others that fell into good soil brought forth fruit, some a hundredfold, another sixty, and another thirty.
Let those who hear, hear.
The disciples came to him and said, "Why do you speak to the people in parables?"
He answered them, "To you it has been given to know the mystery of the kingdom of the heavens; to the ones outside, everything comes in parables.
Anyone who has that kind of knowledge will be given more, indeed much more. But those who do not have that knowledge will have what little they think they have taken away from them.
This is why I use parables when I speak to people. I tell them, "They look but do not see. They listen but do not hear or understand."
In them shall be fulfilled the words of the prophet Isaiah, who said: 'You shall indeed hear, but not understand; and you shall indeed see, but not perceive: for the hearts of this people have grown dull; their ears are hard of hearing, and their eyes have closed; lest they should see with their eyes, and hear with their ears, and understand with their hearts, and should turn for me to heal them.'
The hearts of these people have become callous. Their ears are deaf and their eyes are closed. If they could see with their own eyes, they would see the obvious. And if they could hear with their own ears, they would hear and understand with their hearts. But they have turned back and I cannot heal them. ' (Isaiah 6:9, 10)
May God bless your eyes because you see, and your ears because you hear.
What I am about to tell you is the truth. Many prophets and righteous people wished to see what you are seeing now. But they did not see it. They wished to hear what you are hearing. But they did not hear it.
Listen! This is the meaning of the peasant's story.
People hear the message about the kingdom, but they do not understand it. Then the evil one comes and steals what was sown in their hearts. Those people are like the seed sown along the path.
And others, the rosary which fell upon the rock, and they, when they hear the word, receive it with joy.
But they have no roots. They do not last long. They quickly deviate from the religion when hardship or trial comes because of the message.
And others fell into the good ground, and brought forth fruit, some a hundredfold, some sixtyfold, after they had patiently endured. They hear the word and they eagerly receive it and do not immediately let it go, but they are preoccupied by the anxieties of life and the empty pride of fabricated wealth.
But there are others who have heard the word, and the evil one comes and snatches away what was sown in their hearts. These are the ones who, when they hear the word, immediately receive it with joy. But these, as the days pass, lose their initial enthusiasm and fall away from the Lord. The seed that fell among the thorns represents those who, when they hear the word, allow it to be choked by the worries of this life and the deceitful desires of riches and pleasures. These are the ones who, when they hear the word, allow it to be choked by the worries of this life and the deceitful desires of riches and pleasures.
Jesus spoke another parable to the people and said, "Thus is the kingdom of heaven like a grain of good seed. The man who sowed the good seed is the Son of Man, and the field is the world, and the good seed is the children of the kingdom. The enemy who sowed the tares is the devil, and the field is the world, and the tares are the children of the wicked one. The harvest is the end of the age, and the reapers are the angels. As the tares are gathered and burned in the fire, so will it be at the end of the age. The Son of Man will send out his angels, and they will gather together all who causes others to sin and all evildoers. And they will throw them into the fiery furnace, where there will be weeping and gnashing of teeth. Then the righteous will shine like the sun in the kingdom of their Father. Whoever has ears to hear, let them hear."
And while everyone was sleeping, his enemy came. He sowed weeds among the wheat and then went away.
The wheat began to grow and form grains. At the same time, weeds appeared.
His servants came to him and said, "Sir, did you not sow good seed in your field? Where, then, did the weeds come from?"
He said, "An enemy has done that." The servants asked him, "Do you want us to go and pull up those weeds?"
"No," said the owner of the field. "While you are pulling up the weeds, you might pull up the wheat along with it."
Let both grow together until the harvest. And in the time of the harvest I will say to the reapers, 'Gather first the wheat into my barns.' But the weeds are the children of the evil one. Let them grow together until the harvest. And at the harvest time I will tell the reapers, 'Gather the weeds first and bind them in bundles to burn them; but gather the wheat into my barn.' ' Another parable He put forth, 'The kingdom of heaven is like a mustard seed, which a man took and planted in his field. And it grew and became a large tree, and the birds of the air made nests in its branches.' ' Again He spoke to them in parables, saying, 'The kingdom of heaven is like leaven, which a woman took and hid in three measures of flour, until all was leavened.' ' And Jesus spoke many things to them in parables, saying, 'Behold, a sower went out to sow. And as he sowed, some seeds fell along the path, and the birds came and devoured them. And some seeds fell on rocky ground, where they did not have much soil, and they immediately sprang up and withered away because they had no depth of soil. And some seeds fell among thorns, and the thorns grew up and choked the plants, so they did not bear fruit. And other seeds fell on good ground, and they grew and bore fruit, thirtyfold and sixtyfold and a hundredfold.' ' He also said to them, 'Hearken, the sower went out to sow; and as he sowed, some seed fell beside the path, and was trodden under foot, and the birds of the air devoured it; and other seed fell on the stony places, where they did not have much soil; and they immediately sprang up, and withered away because they had no depth of soil; and other seed fell among the thorns, and the thorns grew up and choked the plants, so they did not bear fruit; and other seed fell on the good ground, and they grew and bore fruit, thirtyfold and sixtyfold and a hundredfold.' ' And He said to them, 'Is a lamp brought in to be put under a basket, or under a bed? Is it not brought in to illuminate the whole house? Let your light shine before men, so that they may see your good works and glorify your heavenly Father.' 'You are the salt of the earth; but if the salt loses its flavor, how can it be made salty again? It is good for nothing, and will be thrown out. Let your speech be always full of grace, seasoned with salt, so that you may know how to answer everyone.' 'Salt is good, but if a small amount of salt loses its salty taste, how can you make it salty again? You will have to throw it away and look for good salt. And don't forget that you are the salt of the earth. If you lose your salty taste, you will be useless and will be thrown away. But if you remain salty, you will add flavor to everything around you and will glorify your heavenly Father. Remember that you are always to be gracious, seasoned with salt, so that you may know how to answer everyone
Jesus said to the crowds another parable: "The kingdom of heaven is like a mustard seed. Which when a man has taken it and planted it in his field, it grows and becomes a great tree, so that the birds of the air come and nest in its branches."
It is the smallest of all your seeds, but when it grows, it becomes the biggest garden plant of all. It becomes a tree. Birds come and rest on its branches.
Jesus told them another parable, saying, "The kingdom of heaven is like leaven, which a woman took and hid in three measures of flour, till the whole was leavened." **Explanation of Translation Choices:** * **"ضرب يسوعُ مثلاً آخرَ لهم قال:"** - "Jesus told them another parable, saying:" * "ضرب مثلاً" is translated as "told a parable." "parable" is the most accurate and common term for "مَثَل" in this context. * "لهم" is translated as "them," referring to Jesus's audience. * "قال" is translated as "saying," indicating that what follows is part of Jesus's speech. * **"مَثَلُ مَملَكةِ السَّمَوات كخَميرَةٍ أخَذَتْها امْرأةٌ وخَلَطَتْها بِثلاثَةِ كَيلَاتٍ مِنَ الدَّقَن."** - "The kingdom of heaven is like leaven, which a woman took and hid in three measures of flour, till the whole was leavened." * **"مَثَلُ مَملَكةِ السَّمَوات"** is translated as "The kingdom of heaven is like..." * "مَملَكةِ السَّمَوات" is translated as "the kingdom of heaven." "Heaven" is a more direct and common translation of "السَّمَوات" in this context than "sky" or "atmosphere." * The phrase "مَثَلُ...خَميرَةٍ" is translated as "is like leaven." "Leaven" is the most accurate and common term for "خَميرَة" in this context. * **"بِثلاثَةِ كَيلَاتٍ مِنَ الدَّقَن."** is translated as "in three measures of flour, till the whole was leavened." * "بِثلاثَةِ كَيلَاتٍ" is translated as "in three measures" or "in three units." "Measures" is a good general term for "كَيلَاتٍ" in this context, although "kilos" or "units" could also be argued depending on the specific type of "kilat" being referred to. In the absence of more specific context, "measures" is a safe and generally accurate translation. * "مِنَ الدَّقَن." is translated as "of flour." "الدَّقَن" is a transliteration of an Arabic word that directly translates to "flour." Therefore, "of flour" is the most accurate and direct translation of "مِنَ الدَّقَن." * **"till the whole was leavened."** This phrase is a more descriptive and understandable way to convey the meaning of the original Arabic text in English. The original Arabic text in this case is a relatively straightforward and literal description of a process. Translating it directly word-for-word would result in a somewhat clunky and less natural-sounding English sentence. Therefore, a slightly more descriptive and natural-sounding English phrasing is used to convey the exact same meaning as the original Arabic text. This approach aims to provide a more fluent and understandable translation for an English-speaking audience, while
Jesus was speaking to the crowds in parables, and without interpreting anything for them. He was not speaking with them.
The word of the prophet who said, "Did I open my mouth and speak? Did I declare the messages of old, since the world was created?" (Psalm 78:2) Jesus illustrates the parable of the tares.
Then he left the crowd and entered the house. The disciples came to him saying, "Explain to us the parable of the tares of the field."
He answered them, "The one who sowed the good seed is the son of man."
The field is the world. The good seed represents the people who belong to the kingdom. The weeds are the people who belong to the evil one.
The enemy who sows them is the devil. And the harvest is the Day of Judgment. And the laborers are the angels.
The good seed is uprooted and burned. Thus, the seed of the wicked is treated on the Day of Judgment.
The Son of Man will send his angels, and they will remove from his kingdom all that causes sin, and they will throw all evildoers out.
They will cast them into the burning furnace. There they will weep and gnash their teeth in anguish.
Then the children of God will shine forth as the sun in the kingdom of their Father. An ear that hears, let it hear. The parables of the hidden treasure and the pearl.
The parable of the Kingdom of Heaven is like a treasure hidden in a field, which a man found and hid. Then, out of joy, he went and sold all that he had and bought that field.
And once again, the Kingdom of the Heavens is like a merchant of pearls who was seeking a fine pearl.
He found one that was very expensive, so he went and sold everything he owned and bought that pearl. The story of the net.
And again, the parable of the kingdom of heaven is like a net cast into the sea, which gathered fish of every kind.
When the net was full, the fishermen drew it to the beach. Then they sat down and took the good fish and put them in the baskets. But the bad fish they threw away.
This is the case on the Day of Judgment. The angels will come. They will separate those who did evil from those who did righteousness.
The evildoers will be thrown into the blazing fire. There they will cry and scream, and they will gnaw their own hands from pain, because of the burning.
Do you understand all these things?" Jesus asked them. And they answered, "Yes."
He said to them: "Every scribe who has been trained for the kingdom of heaven is like a master of a household who brings out from his treasure what is new and what is old." A prophet without honor.
Jesus finished telling these stories, then moved on from there.
He came to his village of Nazareth. He entered the synagogue, teaching in their midst. They were astonished and said, "From where has this man obtained this wisdom and this power, which are manifested in his works?"
Isn't this the son of the carpenter? Isn't his mother called Mary? And his brothers are James, Joseph, Simon, and Judas?
Aren't all his sisters with us? So where did this man get all these things from?
They were never satisfied with him. But Jesus said to them, "A prophet is not without honor, except in his own country and among his own people."
He didn't perform many miracles there because they were not believers.
John the Baptist's head is cut off.
At that time, Herod Antipas, the ruler of Galilee and the territories bordering the wilderness, heard reports about Jesus.
He told his leaders, "This is John the Baptist. He has risen from the dead! That is why he has the power to perform miracles."
Herod arrested John and imprisoned him. He bound him and threw him behind bars because of Herodias. She was married to Philip, Herod's brother.
John said to Herod, "It is not lawful for you to have her as your wife."
Herod wanted to kill John, but he was afraid of the people because they considered him a prophet.
And on Herod's birthday, Herodias's daughter danced before Herod and his guests. And Herod was exceedingly pleased.
So he promised her with an oath that he would give her whatever thing she asked of him.
Her mother said to her, "What are you saying?" The girl said to King Herod, "Give me the head of John the Baptist on a large platter."
The king was very angry. But he remembered his promise and his guests for dinner. So he ordered one of his men to give her what she asked for.
Herod ordered John to be beheaded in prison.
His head was presented on a large platter, and it was offered to the girl, who then carried it to her mother.
The disciples of John came and took his body and buried him. Then they went and told Jesus. Jesus feeds five thousand people.
Jesus heard what had happened to John. He sought solitude and set out in a boat to a deserted place. When the people heard this, they followed him on foot from the neighboring villages.
When Jesus arrived at the shore, he saw a large crowd of people, and he felt compassion for them. And he healed many who were sick and had come to him.
When evening approached, the disciples came to him, saying, "Here is a deserted place, and the hour is already late. Send the crowds away, so that they may go into the villages and buy food for themselves."
Jesus answered, "They do not need to go away. You give them something to eat."
They answered Him, "We have here five loaves of bread and two fish, only."
Bring them here to me.
Then Jesus told the people to sit down on the grass. And He took the five loaves and the two fish, and looking up to heaven, He blessed and broke the loaves and He gave them to the disciples, and they distributed them to the multitude.
Everyone ate and were satisfied. The disciples then gathered twelve baskets of leftover pieces.
There were about five thousand men who ate. Women and children also ate. Jesus walks on water.
When Jesus finished speaking with them, he sent the disciples and instructed them to get into a boat and precede him to the other side of the Sea of Galilee, which is a deserted area.
And when he sent them away, he went up the mountain by himself to pray. And when evening came, he was there alone.
The ship had gone far out to sea; the waves were tossing it about violently because the wind was blowing hard against it.
Jesus went out to the disciples early in the morning. He walked on the water.
They saw him walking on the sea, so they were afraid. They said: A ghost! And they screamed from fright.
Jesus said to them immediately: "Be confident! I am the one. Do not be afraid."
"Master, are you he? Peter asked. If you are he, command me to come to you on the water."
Jesus said, "Come." So Peter got out of the boat, and he walked on the water and headed toward Jesus.
But when Peter saw the wind, he was afraid. And beginning to sink, he cried out, saying, "Lord, save me!"
Jesus touched his eyes with his finger, and the blind man stretched out his hand and immediately regained his sight. Jesus said to him, "Your faith has saved you."
When they climbed into the boat, the wind died down.
Then, those in the boat, having come to the end of their course, and the waves running high, and the wind contrary, and the darkness coming on, and the boat in danger of sinking, and the disciples in great fear, and all hope of salvation gone, and even the light of evening fading, and the darkness of night coming on, and the storm increasing, and the waves rising, and the wind blowing, and the boat tossing, and the disciples crying, and Jesus sleeping, and the Father Almighty permitting all this to come to pass, that the power, and wisdom, and goodness, and justice, and mercy of God might be manifested, and that the Son of God might be exalted, and that the Church might be established, and that the world might be converted, and that the wicked might be punished, and that the righteous might be saved, and that God might be glorified.
They crossed the lake and anchored on the shore near Gencasarit.
The men who lived there recognized Jesus, so they sent word to him in all the neighboring villages. People brought their sick to him from every place.
They entreated Him to touch with the hem of His garment those who were sick. And all who were touched by it were made well.
What makes people "unclean"?
Some Pharisees and some scribes came from Jerusalem to see Jesus; and they asked him,
Why do your disciples not live according to the teachings of the elders? Your disciples don't wash their hands before eating!
Jesus answered, "Why do you not obey the commandment of God to keep your teaching? Who are you?"
The Lord said: "Honor your father and your mother." (Exodus 20:12; Deuteronomy 5:16) And He also said: "Whoever curses his father or his mother, he will be put to death." (Exodus 21:17; Leviticus 20:9)
But you permit people to say to their parents: "Any assistance that you may have received from us is a sacred gift for the sake of God."
So do not force them to honor their parents with it as a gift to them. You make the word of God to be the lowest of the low, so that you may follow what your desires have brought.
O ye who make a claim to faith, even as Isaiah when he cried out concerning you, saying:
These people make me feel old with their words, but their hearts are far away from me.
Their worship has no value in my sight. They teach only man-made doctrines.' (Isaiah 29:13)
Jesus turned to the crowd and said, "Listen and understand."
What enters into your mouth does not defile you, but what comes out of your mouth, that is what defiles you.
Then the disciples met him, saying, "Do you know that the Pharisees were angered when they heard this saying?"
Jesus answered, "There are some who are sons, but who are not from this field. And I am asking the Father to remove them, so that they will not be enemies of the field."
Leave them alone... The Pharisees are guides for the blind. If a blind man leads a blind man, both will fall into a pit.
Peter said, "Then explain this to us."
Haven't you understood yet?
Don't you see, everything that enters the mouth goes to the stomach? Then it comes out of the body.
But the things that come out of the mouth come from the heart. Those are the ones that make you 'unclean'.
Evil thoughts proceed from the heart. So do murder, adultery, and every other sexual immorality. Also, theft, false witness, and slandering people.
Those are the things that make you 'unclean.' Eating without washing your hands, however, does not make anyone unclean.
Jesus left Galilee and went to the region of Sidon and Tyre.
A Canaanite woman who lived in that area near Tyre and Sidon came to him and said to him in a voice filled with both despair and hope: "Lord, Son of David, have mercy on me! My daughter is tormented by a demon and suffers greatly."
Jesus did not utter a word. His disciples approached him and said, "Remove her from us, for she is screaming after us."
Jesus answered, "I was sent only to the lost sheep of Israel."
Then the woman fell on her knees before him. "My lord! Help me!" she said.
He said, "It is not right that we should take the bread of the children and throw it to the young dogs."
Yes, sir, said that woman. But even dogs eat from the crumbs of their masters' tables.
Then Jesus answered, "Woman, great is your faith! Let it be as you want." And her daughter was healed at that hour itself. Jesus feeds the four thousand.
Jesus went away from there. And He passed over the Sea of Galilee. And He went up into a mountain. And He sat there.
Many multitudes came to him. And they brought them to him, blind, and dumb, and maimed, and paralyzed, and those who suffered from many other illnesses and disabilities, unable to walk. And they laid them down at his feet. And he healed them.
The people were amazed. And those who had no ability to speak spoke. And the crippled were healed. And the lame walked. And the blind saw. So the God of Israel was glorified.
Then Jesus called his disciples and said, "I have compassion on the crowd, for they have been with me now for three days and have nothing to eat. And I do not want to send them away hungry, lest they faint on the way."
His disciples answered him, "Here there are only five loaves of bread and two fish, and that is all we have here."
How many loaves do you have? Jesus asked them. They answered him, Seven, and we have a few small fish as well.
Jesus told the multitude to sit down on the ground.
And he took the seven loaves and the ears of grain, and after he had given thanks, he broke them and gave to the disciples, and the disciples gave to the multitude.
Everyone ate and were satisfied. Afterward, the disciples picked up seven baskets full of the leftover bread.
The number of men who ate was four thousand. The women and children also ate.
After Jesus had sent the crowds away, he got into the boat and set off to the district which is called Magadan.
Jesus is asked to perform a miracle.
The Pharisees and Sadducees came, testing Jesus and asking him to show them a sign from heaven.
He answered, saying: "In the evening, look at the sky. It says: "The weather will be fine. The sky is red."
And in the morning she says: "The weather today will be stormy, as the sky is red and overcast with clouds." She knows what she sees in the sky means. But you cannot understand the signs of what is happening now.
A wicked and adulterous generation seeks for a sign, and no sign will be given to it except the sign of Jonah." And He left them and went away. The yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees.
The pupils crossed to the other side of the sea. They forgot to take a loaf of bread with them.
Beware, Jesus said to them. "Beware of the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees."
The pupils talked among themselves, saying, "He must be saying this because we did not take bread with us."
Jesus knows what they are thinking in their hearts. So He said to them, "Why is it you have so little faith? Why are you thinking about these things?"
Haven't you understood yet? Don't you remember the five loaves of bread for the five thousand? How many baskets did you fill with the fragments?
Don't you remember the five bread loaves for the four thousand? How many baskets have you filled with leftovers?
How can you not understand? I did not speak to you about bread. But beware of the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees.
The disciples realized that he had not warned them about the leaven of the Pharisees and Sadducees. And Peter said, "You are the Christ, the Son of the living God."
Jesus went into the region of Caesarea Philippi. There he asked his disciples, "Who do people say the Son of Man is?"
They said, "Some say John the Baptist, others say Elijah, and some say Jeremiah or one of the prophets."
"But who do you say that I am?" he asked them.
Peter answered and said: "You are the Christ, the Son of the living God."
Jesus answered, "Blessed are you, Simon son of Jonah, for flesh and blood has not revealed this to you, but my Father who is in heaven.
This is what I tell you. You are Peter. And upon this rock I will build my church. And the gates of hell shall not prevail against it.
I will give you the keys to the Kingdom of the Heavens. Whatever you bind on Earth will be bound in Heaven, and whatever you loose on Earth will be loosed in Heaven.
Then he instructed the messengers not to disclose his command to anyone.
From that time Jesus began to show His disciples that He must go to Jerusalem, and suffer many things from the elders and chief priests and scribes, and be killed, and rise again on the third day.
Peter took Jesus aside and began to rebuke him. "God forbid, Lord!" he said. "This will never happen to you!"
Jesus turned and said to Peter, "Get behind me, Satan! You are a stumbling block to me; for you are not setting your mind on the things of God, but on the things of men."
Then Jesus said to the disciples: "Whoever wants to come after me must deny themselves and take up their cross and follow me."
If he wants to save his life, he will lose it, but if he loses his life for me, he will find it.
What is the benefit for one who gains the whole world but loses himself? And what can anyone give in exchange for their own soul?
The Son of Man will come in the glory of His Father with His angels, and then He will repay each person according to his works.
Here is the translation of the provided Arabic text: "I am not about to tell you what it is. Some of those who are standing here will not die before they see the Son of Man coming in His kingdom."
Jesus' appearance changed.
And after six days, Jesus took Peter, Jacob, and John his brother, and He led them up to a high mountain by themselves.
And his appearance changed before them, and bright flashes like the sun glistened from his face. And his clothes became dazzling white like light.
And suddenly, Moses and Elijah appeared before them. Moses and Elijah were talking with Jesus.
Peter said to Jesus, "Lord, it is good for us to be here. If you wish, I will make three booths for you. One for you, one for Moses, and one for Aaron."
While he was speaking, a bright cloud overshadowed them. And from the cloud a voice said, "This is my beloved son, in whom I am well pleased. Listen to him."
When the disciples heard this, they were exceedingly amazed and fell to the ground upon their faces before the Lord.
But Jesus went forward and touched them, and said, "Arise, and do not be afraid."
And when they looked, they saw Jesus alone.
They came down from the mountain. And while on the way down the mountain, Jesus told them what to do. He said to them, "Do not tell anyone what you have seen, until the Son of Man is raised from the dead."
The disciples asked him, "Why do the scribes say that Elijah must come first?"
Jesus answered, "It is as you say. For it is necessary for Elijah to come first, and to restore all things."
But I tell you that Elijah has come, and they did not recognize him, but did to him whatever they pleased. So also will the Son of Man be done to."
So the disciples realized that he had been speaking to them about John the Baptist. The healing of a possessed young man
When they came near the crowd, a man approached Jesus and knelt down before him.
Lord, have mercy on my son, for he suffers from violent tremors and is greatly afflicted. He often falls into the fire or into the water.
I brought it to them for your disciples. But they were not able to heal him.
Jesus said: "You unbelieving and evil generation! How long shall I be with you and bear with you? Bring the boy to me."
Jesus commanded the demon to come out of the boy, and immediately it came out. And the boy was healed at that time.
Then the disciples approached Jesus privately and asked him, "Why could we not drive out the demon?"
He said, "Because your faith is very small. What I will tell you now is a reality. If you had faith the size of a mustard seed, it would be enough. You can tell this mountain to move from here to there. And it will move. Nothing will be impossible for you."
